# Rankings: complete boards with local reveal

## Problem and grounding

Show ten ranking rows initially, then reveal ten more on each Show more action in MPs, Individuals, Companies and Parties. Preserve exact backend ordering and financial semantics.

The repository already does the expensive work before truncation: `domain/services/rankings.rs::summarize` aggregates every selected declaration into four maps; `top` sorts each whole map by exact optional decimal amount descending, then identity ascending, and finally takes ten. `Rankings` and its HTTP encoder carry four arrays from one read. `lib/server/rankings.ts` validates identities, duplicates, decimal strings, coverage and period, but additionally rejects boards longer than ten. `+page.server.ts` loads all boards for the selected period and validates category. The page owns category URLs; `rankingRows` derives formatted values, stable category/entity keys and exact bar widths. `RankingList` renders a complete ordered list and numbers rows by index. Updates already uses complete arrays plus a local ten-row reveal.

## Usage (caller's view)

No public endpoint, request parameter or page-load signature is added.

```ts
// Existing server caller remains the only API boundary.
const rankings = await loadRankings(fetch, request.signal, origin, period);
// rankings contains every row in canonical backend order, in all four boards.
```

```ts
// Rankings page: URL owns committed category/period; feature owns reveal state.
const scope = $derived(`${data.period}:${data.selected}`);
let reveal = $state({ scope: '', count: 10 });
const allRows = $derived(data.rankings ? rankingRows(data.rankings, data.selected) : []);
const visibleCount = $derived(reveal.scope === scope ? reveal.count : 10);
const rows = $derived(allRows.slice(0, visibleCount));
const hasMore = $derived(rows.length < allRows.length);

async function showMore(): Promise<void> {
  // TODO: ignore activation while navigating, increment committed scope by ten,
  // clamp to allRows.length, and restore focus if exhaustion removes the button.
  throw new Error('not implemented');
}
```

```svelte
<!-- Existing imported RankingList, with existing neutral Button from the barrel. -->
<div class="board" bind:this={board} tabindex="-1">
  <RankingList {rows} label={existingBoardLabel} />
  {#if hasMore}
    <div class="show-more">
      <Button onclick={showMore} disabled={Boolean(navigating.to)}>Show more</Button>
    </div>
  {/if}
</div>
```

The real implementation should keep existing empty/error branches and labels. The scoped count is intentional: category or period commits immediately derive ten rows without a reactive-effect race. Returning to a previously expanded category starts at ten. On the final reveal, await `tick()` and focus the feature-owned board only if the now-removed button held focus; use `focus({ preventScroll: true })`. This supports parties, whose rows have no anchors, without querying UI component internals. While more rows remain, focus stays on the button.

## Shape and ownership

```rust
// domain/services/rankings.rs
/// Complete board ordered by exact GBP amount descending, null last,
/// then canonical identity ascending. No presentation limit.
fn ordered<K: Ord, I>(groups: BTreeMap<K, (I, PeriodAggregate)>) -> Vec<Ranking<I>> {
    todo!("retain current projection and comparator; remove take(10)")
}

// Existing public service and data signatures remain unchanged.
fn rankings(&self, period: ReportingPeriod)
    -> impl Future<Output = Result<Rankings, RankingsError>> + Send;
// Rankings { period, current_term_start, members, individuals, companies, parties }
```

Backend owns inclusion, aggregation and ordering. Update the top-ten documentation on the service/model and replace the truncation-specific test. Do not alter repository selection, SQL, aggregate behavior, identity rules or serialization. No database migration is needed.

Server parser owns trust in external responses. Remove only `value.length > 10`; retain `Array.isArray`, per-board identity uniqueness, valid IDs, exact decimal strings, count/coverage checks, timestamps and requested-period matching. An arbitrary replacement maximum would silently restore truncation or reject valid datasets. Keep full arrays in the existing typed `Rankings` page model.

The feature owns the presentation batch size (one constant, ten), count, category/period reset, URL behavior, pending state, focus and bar geometry. Compute `rankingRows` against the complete board and slice the presentation rows afterward so the bar scale is invariant across reveals, including negative rows. Never resort or sum on the client. Rank numbers continue naturally because the same list receives the complete visible prefix.

Keep category links and period navigation as they are; optionally remove an irrelevant `page` query parameter when changing category for consistency with PeriodSelector, but do not give it new meaning. Do not replace category navigation with local buttons. Keep existing committed results while navigation is pending, disable Show more for that interval, and reset only when the new committed scope arrives. Failed navigation follows the existing server-load error behavior. Local reveal has no request, pending interval or new failure state, so it needs no retry endpoint, spinner or additional error copy.

Shared UI stays unchanged. Button and RankingList already supply the required capability. Page CSS adds only arrangement (`margin-top: var(--space-4)`); no internal selectors, direct internal imports or hardcoded appearance. The board focus hook belongs to the feature. If a visible board focus ring is required, use documented focus tokens rather than an appearance override on RankingList.

This has a deep interface: the existing service hides aggregation/order, the existing loader hides transport/validation, and the page coordinates only a local visible prefix. No new abstraction is needed for a single count transition. Scope identity and response identity remain distinct: an ordinary same-scope reload need not reset; changing category or period does.

## Financial invariants

Only row availability changes. MPs still include every declaration category, including unknown IDs. Other boards still use DonationSupport and canonical funder kinds, with every non-individual kind in Companies. Registration-period boundaries and historical undated behavior remain in ReportingContext. Null differs from zero; known foreign currencies remain excluded without incomplete coverage; missing GBP amounts and unknown currencies remain incomplete. Exact decimals remain strings in JavaScript, declaration counts remain counts, and existing declared-amount labels and party attribution remain unchanged. This is no authorization to change financial semantics.

## Synthesis decision

Recommend complete sorted boards with local reveal as the smallest sound implementation for this codebase. It directly extends the existing four-board snapshot contract and matches the updates page interaction. Preserve the server-boundary validation and pending-scope behavior from the paging alternative; reject its new request protocol for this requirement.

## Alternatives considered

1. **Complete snapshot plus local reveal (recommended).** Removes backend truncation and the parser's ten-row restriction, then adds one scoped count. All revealed rows belong to one snapshot, so there are no duplicate/missing rows from concurrent imports, request races, append errors or client caches. Public interfaces stay fixed. Main cost is transmitting complete boards to the browser, including the three inactive boards.
2. **Bounded server paging.** Introduce a canonical Rust `RankingCategory` enum and checked page/offset or opaque cursor type, a selected-board page result (`rows`, continuation), a server-side same-origin load endpoint, and client append state keyed by category/period. Sort full aggregates then slice ten plus one to determine exhaustion; boundary parsing rejects unknown categories and invalid limits. A robust result must bind continuation to a snapshot or explicitly handle changed data: an amount/identity cursor alone cannot prevent omissions when totals change between requests. The feature must reject stale responses, deduplicate appropriately, retain old rows on append failure, expose retry, disable concurrent fetches, and reset continuation on scope change. This hides payload bounds but exposes a larger continuation/error protocol. Existing computation still reads and sorts all declarations each time unless storage/cache architecture expands. It loses on scope and complexity today.

Reject a hybrid in which the browser asks for successively larger full boards: it introduces asynchronous failure/race states while retransmitting old rows and repeatedly aggregating the same input. Reject independent per-board snapshots fetched during a single reveal session because the existing one-snapshot guarantee is valuable and cheap to retain.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- Accept complete-board network and SSR page-data size in exchange for one coherent snapshot and no new pagination protocol. The number of funders is not bounded by the ten-row UI; this needs explicit observation before treating the approach as a permanent scale strategy.
- Accept that Show more state is local and resets on category/period changes or full navigation, rather than adding a shareable pagination URL the request does not require.
- Accept focusing the board on final reveal in exchange for supporting non-link party rows without adding a shared UI focus API solely for this interaction.

## Test scope

Rust service: more than twenty identities; assert complete result, descending exact values beyond JavaScript precision, identity tie order, negative values, null last and coverage unchanged. Exercise all four output boards with over-ten identities, not only a standalone sorting helper. Retain existing classification/financial tests.

Frontend parser: accept 0, 10, 11 and 23 valid rows; retain rejection of duplicates (including duplicates beyond row ten), invalid identity, malformed totals, timestamp and period mismatch. Add producer/consumer coverage so backend complete arrays are accepted without financial conversion.

Playwright with 23 distinct rows per category: initial ten; click to twenty; click to twenty-three; button disappears; ranks 11–23 and entity destinations are correct; earlier row keys/content remain stable; no additional API read occurs on reveal; final-button keyboard focus remains meaningful for both linked MP and non-linked party boards. Check category and period reset after expansion, empty/ten/exactly-twenty exhaustion, URL-backed category behavior, pending period navigation retaining committed rows and disabling reveal, and rapid navigation committing only the selected scope. Check bar widths before/after reveal, precise amounts and incomplete indicators, narrow viewport and reduced motion. Existing initial API error remains unchanged. No append-error test is needed because append performs no I/O.

Run applicable Rust tests plus frontend `npm run check`, `npm run build`, and affected Playwright tests. Capture desktop screenshots for expanded MPs and parties on the implementation branch and attach them to the PR per AGENTS.md. Review added strings: only Show more is needed.

## Implementation reconciliation

Design only; no repository changes made. No accepted implementation deviations yet.

## Open questions and risks

How large are complete serialized boards on the actual imported dataset, especially canonical funders, and does that keep page loading within the existing ten-second timeout? Measure real payload size and response latency during implementation; if it is unacceptable, choose the bounded-server alternative deliberately rather than imposing a hidden result cap. This does not block preparing the local-reveal implementation and tests.

## Next implementation step

Change the backend board producer to return complete ordered rows and update parser acceptance/tests together, then implement the page's scoped ten-row reveal.
